    "summary": "Fiery set with standout Brown Eyed Women, China Doll and Loose Lucy",
    "blurb": "The first half delivers tight, well‑paced songs while the second half stays punchy without over‑relying on jam vehicles. Highlights include an incendiary Brown Eyed Women, a gorgeous China Doll, and a swinging Loose Lucy with Jerry’s vocals on fire throughout.",
    "review": "September 18, 1993 at Madison Square Garden proved to be one of the Dead’s most electrifying charity gigs. The band opened with a crisp Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo that let Jerry’s voice shine “ON” from the first line, then moved through Little Red Rooster and Althea before hitting a powerhouse Brown Eyed Women that reviewers called “on fire.”  The set stayed tight—Cassidy and Loose Lucy kept the momentum rolling, and the second set traded the usual jam vehicles for a leaner, high‑energy flow: Iko Iko, Victim Or The Crime into Uncle John’s Band > Corinna, followed by a kickass Drums/Space segment that propelled The Wheel and an inspired All Along The Watchtower into a gorgeous China Doll.  The encore’s Gloria capped the night with pure crowd joy.\nAll three recordings—soundboard, audience, and a decent unknown source—agree the show was “fiery” and “vibrant,” praising Jerry’s searing solos, his vocal fire, and the drums’ extra‑kick energy.  With crystal‑clear instrument separation on the Miller SBD, this performance stands out as a must‑listen snapshot of the Dead’s late‑career spark.\n",
